  "responses": {
    "mission_1.search": "Both of them should produce the same  path length because every movement in the grid has a cost of 1. However, A* will often examine fewer cells because it uses a heuristic to estimate the distance to the goal.\n\n Bfs uses fifo queue, meaning it explores each cell level by level without considering which direction is closer to the goal. A* uses a priority queue and calculates the priority using f = g+h, where g is the cost traveled so far and h is the estimated distance remaining. In my code, BFS uses queue.popleft() to explore the oldest cell, while A* uses heappop() to select the cell with the lowest priority. Therefore, A* can focus its search toward the goal and often expand fewer than BFS while still finding an equally short path. ",
    "mission_1.failure": "On the constrained map, bfs, dfs, and iddfs may produce different results because they explore the grid differently. BFS uses a fifo queue, meaning it explores cells level by level and finds the shortest path when all movements have the same cost. DFS uses a lifo stack, meaning it follows one path as far as possible before backtracking, so it may find a longer path or expand a different number of cells. ",
    "mission_2.prediction": "I predict that A and BFS will finish sooner on the constrained map. I think A* will finish faster than BFS because it uses a heuristic to guide the search toward the goal instead of equally exploring every direction equally. \n",
    "mission_2.performance": "It does support my prediction. ",
    "mission_2.choice": "I would choose A* as my delivery planner because it can use a heuristic to find an efficient path toward the goal.",
    "mission_3.prediction": "",
    "mission_3.comparison": "The first planner did well the second planner did not. ",
    "mission_3.recommendation": "",
    "final.synthesis": "A suitable route for a robot sharing space with people must balance search efficiency, navigation accuracy, route quality, and personal safety. A route should not simply be the shortest path; it should also be reliable, avoid obstacles, maintain distance from people, and allow the robot to reach its destination without any unnecessary delays. ",
    "final.course_reflection": "This activity helped me understand that robotics involves much more than programming robots move from one location to another. I learned how different path-planning algorithms work and how their performance can change depending on the environment. I found it interesting to compare planners on open and constrained maps because it showed me that an algorithm performing well in one environment may not perform on another "
  },
